# Environment variables for Photo Music.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in only the groups for the features
# you work on:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# - Never commit .env.local. Git ignores every .env* file except this one.
# - Every variable is server-only. App code reads them only through the
#   getters in lib/config, which validate a group the first time a feature
#   asks for it. A missing or invalid variable shows up then, as an error
#   naming it.
# - Write a $ in a value as \$, because Next.js expands $NAME references.
# - Keep every value empty in this file. It documents variables; it holds no
#   values.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database: getDatabaseConfig()
# Needed by everything that reads or writes the database: accounts, saved
# photo history, preferences and playlists.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection URL. Required for the database group.
# For local development, start the database with `docker compose up -d --wait`
# and use this URL, which matches the credentials and port in
# docker-compose.yml:
#   postgresql://photo_music:photo_music_dev@localhost:54320/photo_music
# Otherwise get it from your PostgreSQL server or database host.
# Format: a postgres:// or postgresql:// URL,
#   postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE
# Percent-encode special characters in the password, such as $ as %24 and @
# as %40, instead of writing \$: `npm run db:check`, `npm run db:migrate`
# and `npm run test:db` load .env.local with Node's parser, which does not
# unescape \$, so only a percent-encoded URL reads the same there and in
# Next.js. `npm run test:db` also needs a server on this computer and a user
# with the CREATEDB privilege, as the local database has.
DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vision AI: getVisionConfig()
# Needed by photo analysis, which turns a photo into a mood.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Anthropic API key for the hosted vision model (Claude). Required for the
# vision group. Create it in the Claude Console: Console -> API keys.
# Format: one string with no spaces that starts with sk-ant-, such as
#   sk-ant-api03-example-key
VISION_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Spotify: getSpotifyConfig()
# Needed by Spotify search: the featured song and playlist tracks.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Client ID of your Spotify app. Required for the Spotify group.
# Get it from the Spotify for Developers dashboard
# (https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard): create an app, then open its
# Settings.
# Format: 32 hexadecimal characters, such as 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=

# Client secret of the same Spotify app. Required for the Spotify group.
# Get it from the same Settings page ("View client secret").
# Format: 32 hexadecimal characters, such as 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YouTube: getYouTubeConfig()
# Needed by YouTube search: links to music videos.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# YouTube Data API v3 key. Required for the YouTube group. (Issue #18 decides
# whether the app falls back to YouTube search links when it is missing.)
# Get it from the Google Cloud console
# (https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials): enable "YouTube Data
# API v3" for your project, then create an API key.
# Format: 39 characters starting with AIza, such as AIzaSy...
YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Photo storage: getStorageConfig()
# Needed by saved photo history: the private S3-compatible bucket that holds
# registered users' photos. Works with AWS S3, Cloudflare R2 and MinIO.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Endpoint of the S3-compatible service. Optional: leave it empty for AWS S3,
# and set it for Cloudflare R2 or MinIO.
# Get it from the R2 bucket's settings ("S3 API"), or use your MinIO
# server's address.
# Format: an http:// or https:// URL, for example
#   https://ACCOUNT_ID.r2.cloudflarestorage.com (R2)
#   http://localhost:9000 (local MinIO)
S3_ENDPOINT=

# Region of the bucket. Required for the storage group.
# Get it from the bucket's details. Cloudflare R2 uses auto, and MinIO
# uses us-east-1 unless you configured another.
# Format: a region name, such as eu-central-1, auto or us-east-1
S3_REGION=

# Name of the bucket that holds the photos. Required for the storage group.
# Create a private bucket (no public access) in AWS S3, R2 or MinIO.
# Format: a bucket name, such as photo-music-photos
S3_BUCKET=

# Access key ID of a key that can read and write only that bucket. Required
# for the storage group.
# Get it from AWS IAM (an access key for a user limited to the bucket),
# from an R2 API token, or from MinIO's access keys.
# Format: 20 uppercase letters and digits starting with AKIA (AWS), or 32
# hexadecimal characters (R2)
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=

# Secret access key that belongs to S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID. Required for the
# storage group.
# Get it together with the access key ID; it is shown only once.
# Format: 40 letters, digits, / and + (AWS), or 64 hexadecimal characters (R2)
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
